# Cloudflare Pages cache-control config. This project has no build step and
# no content-hashed filenames anywhere (css/style.css, js/*.js, etc. keep the
# same name forever) — so any URL Cloudflare's edge caches with a long TTL
# can silently keep serving stale content for up to that TTL after a real
# deploy changes it, with no filename change to bust the cache. Confirmed
# this exact failure mode on /about after the site restructure: a 7-day
# edge-cached copy of the pre-restructure page kept being served long after
# the file was removed. This blanket rule makes every response cacheable but
# always revalidated against the origin first (matches the max-age=0,
# must-revalidate behavior Cloudflare already applies by default to literal
# .html paths — this just extends it to every path, including the
# extensionless "clean URL" routes that were defaulting to a 7-day s-maxage).
/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate
